    "<![CDATA[The art exhibition &ldquo;Olympic sculpture project Post 88&rdquo; is opened to celebrate 30th of Seoul Olympic and opening of SOMA museum 2.\r\n\r\nArtist : Munseop Shim, Seungteak Lee, Woohwan Lee, and 12 numbers of modern sculpture artist\r\n]]>": 1,
    "<![CDATA[SOMA is holding &lt;The Scenery We Face, Our Daily Gaze&gt;, a special, joint exhibition of Korea and Spain, to offer the chance to view our normal daily sceneries from a different perspective and reconsider their meaning. With the common topic of &ldquo;daily sceneries&rdquo;, we aim to view the daily gaze of artists from Korea and Spain and reflect upon our changing daily lives.\r\n\r\nEven common sceneries from the mundane and repetitive daily lives that we may otherwise pass by are engraved as one of the many impressive sceneries through the perspective and experience of each artist, and these impressions further integrate themselves with the screens to draw yet another impression as a new image. In this exhibition, we can enjoy the various perspectives of six artists, including MyongHi, Kim, YongSun Suh, GeneUk Choi, Jes&uacute;s Sus Monta&ntilde;&eacute;s, Javier Granados, and Jordi Pint&oacute;, on their daily lives through an intersecting form and study different methods of the perspectives on the world of both countries by classifying their works by keywords, including &lsquo;contemplation&rsquo;, &lsquo;capture&rsquo;, &lsquo;phenomenon&rsquo;, &lsquo;observation&rsquo;, &lsquo;pleasure&rsquo;, and &lsquo;imagination&rsquo;.\r\n\r\nMyongHi Kim (1949~) uses oil pastel on blackboards as her formative media, an artistic choice, which evokes childhood memories of school life, to depict various people in their daily lives, such as children, women, and students. From spending her childhood abroad due to her parents&rsquo; careers as diplomats to her daily life travelling back and forth between her workspace in Naepyeong-ri, Chuncheon, that she has renovated from an abandoned school, to Soho, New York, the context overlaps with her thematic consciousness crossing space and time from the past to the present. Her unique method of expression, with clear lighting and shading incorporates an illumination of her human subject brightly against a dark background, casts her gaze upon the person and the object with a quiet, contemplative attitude and brings forth deep contemplations and soul-searching. \r\n\r\nOn the other hand, YongSun Suh (1951~) captures daily sceneries to describe the perspectives and hidden aspects of society that are hidden. Having experienced the urbanization of Seoul firsthand, he expresses various topics, such as self-portraits, cities, nature, history, and myths, in vibrant, reddish colors and forms to continuously study matters related to human existence. He specializes in directly capturing city dwellers in &lt;People on the Street&gt;, &lt;People in the Bus&gt;, and other works of the like. Characters that appear in his work are usually drawn with simple lines lacking any expression, conveying the anonymity of urban sprawl.\r\n\r\nGeneUk Choi (1956~) paints the  phenomena of daily sceneries from a realistic perspective. He endlessly lobs the fundamental question, &ldquo;What is painting?&rdquo;, and subsequently paints realistic sceneries such as those in his neighborhood, subway station, and workspace, in his own style. The extant, realistic sceneries are formed anew with his fast, unrefined, and dynamic touch, as well as a gamut of colors through his view. Although his work may depict the reality that we are facing, it inspires a different emotion and forces us to reflect upon the existence and essence of painting in itself.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe work of Jes&uacute;s Sus Monta&ntilde;&eacute;s (1945~) closely describes the daily lives and common sceneries in Spain. He observes crowds on streets, in markets, at carnivals, and attending festivals in Spain, and then expresses their emotions with passionate lines and colors. The people depicted in his work are not specific individuals, but rather normal people we may each encounter in our own lives. His unique colors are warm and bright, and the natural movement of his subjects emanate vitality through detailed brushstrokes, expressing the peaceful, yet active vistas of Spain.\r\n\r\n\r\nJavier Granados (1964~), who majored in veterinary science but is currently an active artist, understands objects intuitively and expresses them pleasingly with robust colors and unassuming shapes. The artist illustrates the daily life of a woman in his own witty and humorous way, and his voluminously exaggerated characters are quirkily lovely. Though he is simply portraying a woman who is having an ordinary day, carrying out activities such as remaining at home, dancing, or swimming, through the robustly-hued backgrounds contrasting with the character sketched with simple lines, he dramatically emphasizes the character and breathes new life into the mundane to create something exceptional. \r\n\r\n\r\nMeanwhile, the work of Jordi Pint&oacute; (1968~) representing the keyword imagination, replaces the normal sceneries in our daily lives with imaginative utopias. The characters that appear in his work are predominantly families and lovers who may be commonly viewed in our daily lives. However, by daringly skipping their facial features and simplifying their overall shapes, it becomes scenery of a different world, far removed from reality. He is describing ordinary days&rsquo; events, such as dinner with families, reunions with loved ones, and taking a walk, yet he is also providing us with pleasant and blissful experiences as though traveling through an imaginary world life with warm colors, unrealistic backgrounds, and luminous skies.\r\n\r\nThrough the different viewpoints of the six artists from Korea and Spain on the expressions of our daily lives, we can come face to face with daily sceneries that are ordinary, yet special. The daily lives we are now reshaping will belike paintings that can re-create our lives as new scenery. Through our experienced daily lives of yesterday and today, we can re-create novel daily lives in our hearts that will be drawn anew.\r\n]]>": 1,
    "<![CDATA[내일전_Drag and Draw \r\n김인영, 류정민, 민정See, 배성희, 손민아, 심아빈, 이성미, 이정배, 임정은, 장지은\r\n2021.8.13-2022.2.6\r\n소마미술관 1관 Seoul Olympic Museum of Art\r\n주최 주관 국민체육진흥공단 소마미술관\r\n]]>": 1,
    "<![CDATA[MOVE SOMA 1 드로잉 소장품\r\n2020.11.02 ~ 2021.02.15\r\n주최 국민체육진흥공단 주관 소마미술관\r\nMOVE SOMA란? 소마미술관 온라인 가상전시공간\r\n\r\n]]>": 1,
    "<![CDATA[The Seoul Olympic Museum of Art (SOMA) has routinely held exhibitions titled Artists Rediscovered, the first of which was Jooho Kim, Hanjin Park, Kunyong Lee &mdash;Restless Hand, Unfixed Spirit. These exhibitions including Sunghy Shin&mdash;Soonja Han (2009) and TchahSup Kim, Soocheon Jheon, Ai Kyu Hahn &mdash;Deep Breath (2014) have been continuously organized in the form of a two-man or three-man exhibition. This type of art show has given prominence to both the spirit of artists and the spirit of the times through works by artists who are deemed worthy of having their art re-examined from the perspective of contemporary Korean art trends. Such an exhibition was also designed to take a closer look at an artist&rsquo;s worldview and art world in the form of a retrospective from Changbae Hwang &mdash;The Tent of Delights in 2018. \r\n\r\nThis year&rsquo;s Artists Rediscovered exhibition is intended to lay out the story of In Ryu (July 20, 1956-January 11, 1999), a sculptural genius who died at a young age. 2019 marks 20 years since he has passed. In the winter of 1999, a roaring artistic soul died away in a flurry. A graduate of sculpture from Hongik University and its graduate school, Ryu gained a reputation as a distinguished sculptor of figurative Korean sculpture while tackling the human body in a traditional manner coupled with contemporary expression. While accomplishing his artistic feats, he passed away at the young age of 43. He left behind approximately 70 sculptures and installations that were made over the course of 10 years, a relatively short period in terms of artistic activities. This exhibition comprehensively covers the entirety of his artistic practices. It spans 15 years from the period when he first began his artistic activities in earnest with his winning of a special prize at the Korean Mokwoohoe Fine Art Association Contest in 1983 to the suspension of his work in 1997 due to bad health. It also features diverse archived materials that provide a glimpse of his concerns about and experimentations with the themes he had delved into throughout his life. \r\nIn short, the exhibition consists of five themes that pigeonhole In Ryu&rsquo;s works: From Clay, Entering the Mountains&mdash;Descending the Mountains, The Stage of Life, Portraits of Contemporaries, and The Spirit of a Sculptor. The artist expressed his ideas about his work through his interviews with the press and contributions to the media over the course of his lifetime. His statements, which could be references for each theme, were extracted from his interviews to help understand and appreciate his pieces. If we were to appreciate his works while reflecting on his statements, his serious and blind aspiration for sculpture would be conveyed by the way it deeply resonates with our hearts. On display in the archive space are various aspects of his life and art as exemplified by multiple types of materials, such as drawings and maquettes for coming up with works, notes that showcase his studies of anatomy, replicas of his hand made before and after his death, books and newspapers he referred to while working, and news articles and TV broadcasting videos featuring his life and art. \r\n]]>": 1,
    "<![CDATA[&lt; MOVE SOMA 2_메타파크 &gt;전시 바로가기=&gt; https://soma.kspo.or.kr/online/art/plan/88]]>": 1,
